 "ld1-1646149545906-1646150103551-0": {
  "id": "ld1-1646149545906-1646150103551-0",
  "title": "Excursion aux grottes de Samoun ou des crocodiles : haute Égypte / dessins de Karl Girardet",
  "unitCode": "SIL",
  "type": "edanmdm",
  "url": "edanmdm:siris_sil_761567",
  "content": {
   "freetext": {
    "date": [
     {
      "label": "Date",
      "content": "1862"
     }
    ],
    "name": [
     {
      "label": "Author",
      "content": "Georges, A"
     },
     {
      "label": "Author",
      "content": "Girardet, Karl 1813-1871"
     },
     {
      "label": "Author",
      "content": "Smithsonian Libraries African Art Index Project DSI"
     }
    ],
    "notes": [
     {
      "label": "Notes",
      "content": "Illustrations"
     },
     {
      "label": "Notes",
      "content": "Georges describes the landscape and scenery between Meguel-Qual and the Arabic mountain range under which are the Samoun caves. He relates his arrival at Daklé and the legends of the place. He describes how he entered the caves and his discoveries of the embalmed crocodiles and all kinds of mummies (human, birds, reptiles). He relays the old story of the four Maghrebians who were looking for gold in the caves and had a vision of a naked woman who would have been a \"djinn,\" responsible for the underground treasures. The author was curious about the origin of the burials found in the caves of Samoun. Were the caves used as graves for ordinary people? The gold found around the feet and the hands of many mummies suggests not."
     },
     {
      "label": "Notes",
      "content": "Includes illustration of the interior of the grotto with human and crocodile mummies."
     }
    ],
